# ━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
# TOON Linode devnet — nginx reverse proxy (template).
# ━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
# `${DOMAIN}` is the ONLY placeholder; bootstrap.sh renders this to
# nginx/conf.d/devnet.conf with:  envsubst '${DOMAIN}' < template > conf.d/devnet.conf
# (the single-var filter leaves nginx's own $host/$http_upgrade/... untouched).
#
# Backends are referenced through the `$backend` variable + the Docker embedded
# resolver (127.0.0.11) so nginx boots even if a chain container is briefly down
# or still starting (lazy, per-request resolution instead of boot-time).

# Per-IP rate limits for the public RPC/faucet endpoints (devnet abuse guard).
limit_req_zone $binary_remote_addr zone=rpc:10m rate=30r/s;

# Looser per-IP limit for the PAID connector edge (issue #222). A paid round-trip
# is a burst of paid PREPAREs (POST /ilp) plus the inner reverse-proxied write; the
# real cost gate is the on-chain payment-channel claim, NOT nginx — so the /ilp edge
# gets its own, more generous zone than the 30 r/s RPC one.
limit_req_zone $binary_remote_addr zone=ilp:10m rate=100r/s;

# WebSocket upgrade plumbing (used by the Solana WS endpoint; harmless elsewhere).
map $http_upgrade $connection_upgrade {
    default upgrade;
    ''      close;
}

# Subdomain -> chain backend (Docker service name + port on the shared network).
# connector/relay-ws are the issue #222 app-behind-connector edge: nginx reaches
# them by SERVICE NAME over the compose network, so their host ports staying on
# 127.0.0.1 is fine. The app's PAID-write store port (3100) and the connector's
# admin API (8081) are deliberately ABSENT here — they must stay internal (AC2).
map $host $backend {
    default               "";
    evm-rpc.${DOMAIN}     "http://anvil:8545";
    solana-rpc.${DOMAIN}  "http://solana-validator:8899";
    solana-ws.${DOMAIN}   "http://solana-validator:8900";
    faucet.${DOMAIN}      "http://faucet:3500";
    connector.${DOMAIN}   "http://connector:3000";
    relay-ws.${DOMAIN}    "http://app:7100";
}

# ── Port 80: ACME challenge + redirect everything else to HTTPS ──────────────
server {
    listen 80;
    listen [::]:80;
    server_name evm-rpc.${DOMAIN} solana-rpc.${DOMAIN} solana-ws.${DOMAIN}
                faucet.${DOMAIN} mina.${DOMAIN}
                connector.${DOMAIN} relay-ws.${DOMAIN};

    location /.well-known/acme-challenge/ {
        root /var/www/certbot;
    }
    location / {
        return 301 https://$host$request_uri;
    }
}

# ── Port 443: chain RPC / WS / faucet (one block, host-routed) ───────────────
server {
    listen 443 ssl;
    listen [::]:443 ssl;
    http2 on;
    server_name evm-rpc.${DOMAIN} solana-rpc.${DOMAIN} solana-ws.${DOMAIN} faucet.${DOMAIN};

    ssl_certificate     /etc/letsencrypt/live/evm-rpc.${DOMAIN}/fullchain.pem;
    ssl_certificate_key /etc/letsencrypt/live/evm-rpc.${DOMAIN}/privkey.pem;
    ssl_protocols TLSv1.2 TLSv1.3;

    resolver 127.0.0.11 valid=10s ipv6=off;

    location / {
        if ($backend = "") { return 404; }       # unknown subdomain
        limit_req zone=rpc burst=60 nodelay;

        proxy_pass $backend;
        proxy_http_version 1.1;
        proxy_set_header Host $host;
        proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-For $proxy_add_x_forwarded_for;
        proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-Proto https;

        # WebSocket upgrade (Solana WS subscriptions on solana-ws.*).
        proxy_set_header Upgrade $http_upgrade;
        proxy_set_header Connection $connection_upgrade;
        proxy_read_timeout 1h;

        client_max_body_size 4m;                  # JSON-RPC batch ceiling
    }
}

# ── Port 443: Mina — passthrough to the PUBLIC devnet (no local node) ────────
server {
    listen 443 ssl;
    listen [::]:443 ssl;
    http2 on;
    server_name mina.${DOMAIN};

    ssl_certificate     /etc/letsencrypt/live/evm-rpc.${DOMAIN}/fullchain.pem;
    ssl_certificate_key /etc/letsencrypt/live/evm-rpc.${DOMAIN}/privkey.pem;
    ssl_protocols TLSv1.2 TLSv1.3;

    resolver 1.1.1.1 8.8.8.8 valid=300s;

    location /graphql {
        limit_req zone=rpc burst=60 nodelay;
        set $mina_upstream "https://api.minascan.io";
        proxy_pass $mina_upstream/node/devnet/v1/graphql;
        proxy_ssl_server_name on;
        proxy_set_header Host api.minascan.io;
        proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-For $proxy_add_x_forwarded_for;
    }
    location / { return 404; }                     # only proxy /graphql
}

# ── Port 443: app-behind-connector (issue #222) — PAID edge + free WS reads ─
# Two public faces of the #221 app profile:
#   connector.${DOMAIN}   → connector:3000  (POST /ilp — PAID writes enter here)
#   relay-ws.${DOMAIN}    → app:7100        (free Nostr WS reads, bypass the paid edge)
# nginx dials both BY SERVICE NAME over the compose network, so their host ports
# staying on 127.0.0.1 is fine. The app's paid-write store port (3100) and the
# connector admin API (8081) are NOT proxied anywhere here — they stay internal
# (AC2: paid writes MUST flow through the connector; admin stays private).
server {
    listen 443 ssl;
    listen [::]:443 ssl;
    http2 on;
    server_name connector.${DOMAIN} relay-ws.${DOMAIN};

    ssl_certificate     /etc/letsencrypt/live/evm-rpc.${DOMAIN}/fullchain.pem;
    ssl_certificate_key /etc/letsencrypt/live/evm-rpc.${DOMAIN}/privkey.pem;
    ssl_protocols TLSv1.2 TLSv1.3;

    resolver 127.0.0.11 valid=10s ipv6=off;

    location / {
        if ($backend = "") { return 404; }       # unknown subdomain
        # Paid edge gets the looser `ilp` zone; the on-chain claim is the real gate.
        limit_req zone=ilp burst=200 nodelay;

        proxy_pass $backend;
        proxy_http_version 1.1;
        proxy_set_header Host $host;
        proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-For $proxy_add_x_forwarded_for;
        proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-Proto https;

        # WebSocket upgrade (relay-ws.* free Nostr WS reads).
        proxy_set_header Upgrade $http_upgrade;
        proxy_set_header Connection $connection_upgrade;
        proxy_read_timeout 1h;

        # POST /ilp carries a full inner HTTP envelope (request line + headers +
        # body) inside the ILP PREPARE `data`, so allow a larger body than the
        # JSON-RPC ceiling.
        client_max_body_size 16m;
    }
}
